Mastodon

A Plug-In for Mill. Put it in the "Mill Plug-Ins" folder.

It reads Mastodon's client API (JSON over HTTPS) and shows what it
reads as pages: timelines, accounts, hashtags, and threads. Other
servers that serve the same API (GoToSocial, Pleroma, Akkoma, snac,
and the like) work too. It doesn't speak ActivityPub itself.

Addresses

  mastodon://instance/              the instance's local timeline
  mastodon://instance/public        its federated timeline
  mastodon://instance/home          your home timeline (logged in)
  mastodon://instance/tags/name     a hashtag's posts
  mastodon://instance/@user         an account's posts (@user@other
                                    for one from another instance)
  mastodon://instance/@user/id      a post, with what it replies to
                                    above it and its replies below
  mastodon://instance/login         log in or out

Any of these but a post's takes ?max_id=id, as the "Next page" link
at the foot of a page does. /public/local is the same as /. A post's
address is the one its instance shows on the web, with mastodon: for
https: (https://mastodon.social/@user/123 is
mastodon://mastodon.social/@user/123).

Pages

Each post shows its author, its date and time (UTC), and its text:
paragraphs, line breaks, links, bold, italic, code, quotes, and lists
are kept; anything else is shown as plain text. Hashtags and mentions
link to their mastodon: pages. A boost says who boosted it; a reply
links to the post it answers. A content warning hides the post behind
a "Show" link, which opens it on its own page. Pictures and other
attachments are links (or, with Show pictures on, small pictures that
link to the whole one); four are shown at most. A post's text is cut
at 6K.

HTTPS

Mill can't make HTTPS connections, so with Use HTTPS on (as it is at
first) every request goes to Mill's HTTP proxy, set in Settings under
HTTP, which makes the HTTPS connection, as for Mill's own https:
pages. The proxy has to pass the API's replies on as they are: one
that rewrites pages for old browsers (MacProxy does) can't carry
them, and Mill says so. With Use HTTPS off, the requests go straight
to the instance over plain HTTP, for a server on your own network.

Logging in

Public timelines need no login, where the instance allows them. To
see your home timeline, post, favorite, or boost, make an access
token: in the instance's settings, under Development, make a New
Application with the read and write scopes, open it, and copy "Your
access token". Paste it into mastodon://instance/login. It's checked
with the instance, then kept in Mill's settings (in the Mill Settings
file, as it is), and sent only to that instance, through the proxy.
One instance has a token at a time. Pages never show it. Log Out
forgets it; revoke it in the instance's settings to end it there.

Logged in, the instance's pages have a form to post (with a content
warning and who sees it), each post has Favorite and Boost buttons,
and a post's own page has a form to reply. These work only from the
Plug-In's own pages.

Settings

Under Mastodon, in Settings, Protocols:

  Use HTTPS       through the HTTP proxy (on at first)
  Posts a page    1 to 40 (10 at first)
  Show pictures   small pictures in posts (off at first: links)

Memory

About 20K while a page is read, given back at its end. Replies are
read as they come, never kept whole, so a long timeline takes no more.

Notes

  - Times are UTC, as the API gives them.
  - Text is shown in Mac Roman; characters it lacks are shown as "?".
  - What you post is sent as Mill sends any form from a Mac Roman
    page, in ISO 8859-1, then made UTF-8: Mac characters that Latin-1
    lacks (curly quotes, dashes) become "?".
  - Polls, cards, and custom emoji pictures aren't shown; a custom
    emoji is its :name:.
  - A thread page asks the instance three times: what came before, the
    post, and its replies.

Build: ./build.sh writes build/Mastodon.bin. Tests: tests/run.sh (the
JSON reader, posts' HTML, addresses, and pages) and tests/e2e.sh (the
Plug-In against a fake instance, tests/fakemasto.py).
